  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through Friday)\nIssued at 1222 PM MDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nMonday begins the long term period with continued rounds of fast \nmoving showers with embedded thunderstorms flowing northeastward \nacross western NM and into the Rio Grande Valley. This continues \nthrough Tuesday with a notable dryslot continuing to shutdown \nprecipitation chances along and east of the central mountain chain. \nDifferences begin to arise amongst global numerical model suites \nwith the evolution of the troughing pattern closing off either to \none or two distinct low pressure systems over NV and CA. This \ndoesn't have much impact on the forecast sensible weather conditions \nover NM through Tuesday, but does end up causing large downstream \ndivergences amongst various model runs. Whichever direction the \nupper low ends up taking, the remnants of Hurricane Simon favor a \nprogression over the TX Big Bend area, clipping southeastern NM \nWednesday.\n\nThe 12Z GFS, many ensemble means and AI runs of the GFS and ECMWF \nall favor the H5 closed low opening and being more progressive \nWednesday into Thursday, trekking eastward over CO. This setup \nfeatures a classic Fall cold front sweeping west to east across the \nforecast area bringing cooler and drier conditions in its wake \nWednesday night into Thursday morning. The backdoor portion of the \ncold front sweeps south and west thru eastern NM thereafter Thursday \nnight into Friday, replenishing low level moisture. The currently \nless likely scenario advertised by the 00Z runs of the ECMWF and \nCanadian have the H5 low cutting off and retrograding back toward \nnorthern AZ or further west. This brings a surface cold front \nbacking into eastern NM late week further west with higher moisture \ncontent behind it advecting in from the southern Great Plains. More \nwidespread precipitation chances break out across mostly the central \nand eastern NM as a result in this scenario. It's a lower \nprobability scenario at this time, but worth mentioning given the \nsharp change in forecast weather pattern it would bring.",
